WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.690 --> 00:00:05.756
My name is Jon Bristol,

00:00:05.756 --> 00:00:07.330
and I'm from Trinidad and Tobago.

00:00:07.330 --> 00:00:08.738
I'm the director of Super Fresh,

00:00:08.738 --> 00:00:10.171
and today I'm going to show you

00:00:10.171 --> 00:00:12.260
how we clean very dirty mattresses

00:00:12.260 --> 00:00:14.263
as well as the average dirty ones.

00:00:16.580 --> 00:00:19.892
So, a typical process of
cleaning a mattress is

00:00:19.892 --> 00:00:22.480
we spray on an enzyme-based cleaner,

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:24.022
and that will break down the dirt

00:00:24.022 --> 00:00:25.147
and the body oils

00:00:25.147 --> 00:00:27.220
and, you know, the incidents.

00:00:27.220 --> 00:00:29.390
And we give that chemical
some time to work.

00:00:29.390 --> 00:00:31.380
After that, there's some agitation,

00:00:31.380 --> 00:00:33.013
also known as scrubbing,

00:00:33.013 --> 00:00:34.704
and in the final stage of the process,

00:00:34.704 --> 00:00:35.880
we use the extractor,

00:00:35.880 --> 00:00:38.670
and that would spray on a
solution of clean water,

00:00:38.670 --> 00:00:40.536
fabric shampoo, and deodorizer

00:00:40.536 --> 00:00:41.752
and vacuum at the same time.

00:00:41.752 --> 00:00:43.560
So it rinses away everything,

00:00:43.560 --> 00:00:45.768
so there isn't any harsh chemicals left

00:00:45.768 --> 00:00:47.293
to interfere with anyone's skin

00:00:47.293 --> 00:00:48.501
or anything afterwards.

00:00:50.070 --> 00:00:53.110
There's going to be a lot
of dust buildup over time.

00:00:53.110 --> 00:00:54.680
There's going to be hair.

00:00:54.680 --> 00:00:56.230
If there are a lot of kids in your home,

00:00:56.230 --> 00:00:57.500
you're going to find urine,

00:00:57.500 --> 00:00:58.840
even if the client doesn't know.

00:00:58.840 --> 00:01:00.065
Sometimes, you know,

00:01:00.065 --> 00:01:01.673
some people eat on their beds and stuff,

00:01:01.673 --> 00:01:04.268
so you find crumbs from various foodstuff.

00:01:09.970 --> 00:01:13.086
All right. So, I actually had no idea

00:01:13.086 --> 00:01:15.480
what to expect when coming to do this job.

00:01:15.480 --> 00:01:18.490
And I'm still not entirely
sure what caused it.

00:01:18.490 --> 00:01:21.610
It may have been some
flooding in the area,

00:01:21.610 --> 00:01:22.600
but I'm not sure.

00:01:22.600 --> 00:01:25.233
So that was probably more
than five years for sure.

00:01:26.820 --> 00:01:28.770
So, right here, I am spraying on

00:01:28.770 --> 00:01:31.136
the enzyme-based cleaner
onto the mattress,

00:01:31.136 --> 00:01:32.610
and I'm looking to see the areas

00:01:32.610 --> 00:01:35.863
where I may have to apply
another coat as we go along.

00:01:37.550 --> 00:01:40.740
What I use right now,
it's a synthetic enzyme.

00:01:40.740 --> 00:01:41.926
I could use it for everything,

00:01:41.926 --> 00:01:42.883
but there are some stains

00:01:42.883 --> 00:01:45.188
that would require additional chemicals

00:01:45.188 --> 00:01:47.280
if you really want to get
them removed visually.

00:01:47.280 --> 00:01:49.810
But the most important
thing for most clients

00:01:49.810 --> 00:01:51.876
is getting the actual thing

00:01:51.876 --> 00:01:53.632
that caused the stain out.

00:01:53.632 --> 00:01:55.181
So we say there may not be

00:01:55.181 --> 00:01:57.505
a visual improvement, but you can be sure

00:01:57.505 --> 00:01:59.632
that you're getting all the nasties out.

00:02:01.100 --> 00:02:03.380
They're usually just regular buildup,

00:02:03.380 --> 00:02:06.020
so just regular dust and dirt

00:02:06.020 --> 00:02:07.700
that accumulate over the years.

00:02:07.700 --> 00:02:09.933
Some clients don't use

00:02:09.933 --> 00:02:11.860
a full three-piece sheet set.

00:02:11.860 --> 00:02:12.901
A lot of clients don't have

00:02:12.901 --> 00:02:14.834
mattress protectors either.

00:02:14.834 --> 00:02:17.555
Using heat is beneficial because

00:02:17.555 --> 00:02:19.650
heat acts almost like
a universal catalyst.

00:02:19.650 --> 00:02:22.350
So heat would help your
chemicals to work faster,

00:02:22.350 --> 00:02:23.655
so it would help the enzyme cleaner

00:02:23.655 --> 00:02:25.430
to break down the stuff faster,

00:02:25.430 --> 00:02:26.971
and it will also help the fabric shampoo

00:02:26.971 --> 00:02:29.210
to latch onto the dirt particles faster.

00:02:29.210 --> 00:02:31.890
So it helps make the cleaning
process more efficient.

00:02:31.890 --> 00:02:35.140
Unfortunately, there's
no way to know for sure

00:02:35.140 --> 00:02:37.255
that you get 100% of it,

00:02:37.255 --> 00:02:38.438
but you want to know
that you're picking up

00:02:38.438 --> 00:02:39.897
as much as possible.

00:02:44.400 --> 00:02:46.500
This mattress was especially dirty,

00:02:46.500 --> 00:02:48.250
so it's either, like, a lot of years,

00:02:48.250 --> 00:02:49.970
probably more than seven or so,

00:02:49.970 --> 00:02:51.578
or a lot of use.

00:02:51.578 --> 00:02:53.370
But I don't think it's any one thing,

00:02:53.370 --> 00:02:55.184
but just a combination of factors

00:02:55.184 --> 00:02:57.390
that leaves it to be that condition.

00:02:57.390 --> 00:03:00.160
I found that you could use
a variable-speed polisher

00:03:00.160 --> 00:03:02.150
and a scrubbing-brush attachment.

00:03:02.150 --> 00:03:05.380
The brush is rotating at about 600 rpm.

00:03:05.380 --> 00:03:07.321
We use the softest brush available,

00:03:07.321 --> 00:03:09.529
because some fabrics are really delicate.

00:03:09.529 --> 00:03:11.307
And we use that to help break down

00:03:11.307 --> 00:03:13.165
the larger debris and stuff

00:03:13.165 --> 00:03:14.656
on the surface of the fabric.

00:03:14.656 --> 00:03:17.470
So it helps break down the
larger pieces of debris,

00:03:17.470 --> 00:03:19.055
'cause sometimes they have buildup

00:03:19.055 --> 00:03:20.321
that is really, really bad,

00:03:20.321 --> 00:03:23.000
and it's actually, like, caked up.

00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:25.910
The general process for removing stains

00:03:25.910 --> 00:03:28.076
is just to isolate where the stain is,

00:03:28.076 --> 00:03:31.009
and I may double-apply the
enzyme-based cleaner to it

00:03:31.009 --> 00:03:33.650
or I may spend a little
longer scrubbing it.

00:03:33.650 --> 00:03:36.741
[water gushing]

00:03:36.741 --> 00:03:39.574
[cleaner buzzing]

00:03:41.130 --> 00:03:42.900
I believe for this one the client told us

00:03:42.900 --> 00:03:44.575
it was probably about two years,

00:03:44.575 --> 00:03:46.820
and it wasn't too bad.

00:03:46.820 --> 00:03:49.370
It was just, like, a cream-ish color,

00:03:49.370 --> 00:03:51.090
but we know the original color was white,

00:03:51.090 --> 00:03:52.798
and so we sprayed it one time

00:03:52.798 --> 00:03:54.770
and we did the scrubbing.

00:03:54.770 --> 00:03:56.290
I mean, you'll always have the extreme.

00:03:56.290 --> 00:03:58.960
Some would be much, much darker brown.

00:03:58.960 --> 00:04:00.740
Some would be much more spotty.

00:04:00.740 --> 00:04:03.410
But the average person
is just a little cream,

00:04:03.410 --> 00:04:06.580
just a little off-white
from where it should be.

00:04:06.580 --> 00:04:08.330
For mattresses, you're
usually going to get

00:04:08.330 --> 00:04:09.530
those kind of colors,

00:04:09.530 --> 00:04:11.790
because even below the surface,

00:04:11.790 --> 00:04:12.948
you still have a lot of debris.

00:04:12.948 --> 00:04:14.064
Your dead skin cells,

00:04:14.064 --> 00:04:15.810
your dirt, and your dust building up.

00:04:15.810 --> 00:04:17.726
So even if the top surface

00:04:17.726 --> 00:04:20.109
doesn't accurately
represent what is hidden

00:04:20.109 --> 00:04:21.630
inside there, you're going to find it

00:04:21.630 --> 00:04:23.472
once we do the extraction.

00:04:23.472 --> 00:04:25.008
[water gushing]

00:04:30.738 --> 00:04:32.140
After a certain amount of years,

00:04:32.140 --> 00:04:35.620
your mattress significantly
increases in weight

00:04:35.620 --> 00:04:37.310
simply due to the dust

00:04:37.310 --> 00:04:39.093
and dead skin cells that collect.

00:04:39.093 --> 00:04:41.280
So, one, it's going to
be more uncomfortable.

00:04:41.280 --> 00:04:43.050
Two, if you're using it regularly,

00:04:43.050 --> 00:04:45.360
some of that is going
to be entering the air.

00:04:45.360 --> 00:04:48.052
So you're going to have
a lower-quality air

00:04:48.052 --> 00:04:50.295
inside the home. It may attract mites

00:04:50.295 --> 00:04:52.330
if there's a lot of moisture around.

00:04:52.330 --> 00:04:53.930
Just overall it's not going to be

00:04:53.930 --> 00:04:55.529
the best way of well-being.

00:04:55.529 --> 00:04:57.150
For people that are asthmatic,

00:04:57.150 --> 00:04:59.400
the buildup of dust could be a problem.

00:04:59.400 --> 00:05:01.340
For people with sensitive skin,

00:05:01.340 --> 00:05:03.370
any buildup on your mattress over time

00:05:03.370 --> 00:05:04.910
could pose a threat in the future

00:05:04.910 --> 00:05:06.757
in terms of having flare-ups.

00:05:06.757 --> 00:05:09.500
If you're taking only
preventative measures,

00:05:09.500 --> 00:05:11.157
I don't think you should need to service

00:05:11.157 --> 00:05:12.798
more than, like, once a year.

00:05:12.798 --> 00:05:15.160
Get it cleaned and then
get a mattress protector,

00:05:15.160 --> 00:05:16.405
because you can always wash that

00:05:16.405 --> 00:05:17.788
every fortnight or every month,

00:05:17.788 --> 00:05:19.221
and you're in a much better position

00:05:19.221 --> 00:05:22.042
than paying me. And I don't mind,

00:05:22.042 --> 00:05:23.430
but you're in a much better position

00:05:23.430 --> 00:05:24.580
for your own well-being.

